claim.claim-text:
capturing at least one first image of the user taken with a camera located at a first distance from the user, the camera associated with a computing device;
processing the at least one first image or a portion thereof to create first biometric data,
capturing, at the second distance, at least one second image of the user taken with the camera associated with the computing device, such that the second distance is different from the first distance;
processing the at least one second image, or a portion thereof, to create second biometric data,
processing the first biometric data and the second biometric data to determine whether expected differences exist between the first biometric data and the second biometric data to indicate an expected type of distorting change in at least one image that is consistent with a real person being imaged and which is indicative of three-dimensionality; and
determining the user is not three-dimensional when the first biometric data and the second biometric data do not have the expected differences indicating the user exhibits three-dimensionality.

capturing one or more additional images at distances from the user that are between the first distance and the second distance;
for at least one of the one or more additional images, generating additional biometric data;
examining the additional biometric data, the first biometric data, and the second biometric data, or portions thereof, to determine whether expected differences therebetween indicate the user exhibits three-dimensionality.

receive first biometric data or derive the first biometric data from at least one first image, of a user's face, captured with a camera located at a first distance from the user's face;
receive the second biometric data or derive the second biometric data from at least one second image, of the user's face, captured with the camera located at a second distance from the user's face, the second distance being different than the first distance;
processing the first biometric data and second biometric data to determine whether expected differences exist between the first biometric data and the second biometric data to indicate an expected type of distorting change in at least one image that is consistent with a real person being imaged and which is indicative of three-dimensionality; and
determining the user is not three-dimensional when the first biometric data and the second biometric data do not have the expected differences indicating the user exhibits three-dimensionality.

receive first biometric data, or derive first biometric data, from at least one first image of the person's face captured with a camera located at a first distance from the person's face;
receive second biometric data, or derive second biometric data, from at least one second image of the person captured with the camera located at a second distance from the person's face, the second distance being different than the first distance;
process the first biometric data and the second biometric data to determine whether expected differences exist between the first biometric data and the second biometric data to indicate an expected type of distorting change in at least one image that is consistent with a real person being imaged and which is indicative of three-dimensionality,
determining the user is not three-dimensional when the first biometric data and the second biometric data do not have the expected differences indicating the user exhibits three-dimensionality.
